  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-108567",
      "knowledge_point_id": "88580aff-e043-58ea-a8ca-1ce32026428f",
      "knowledge_point": "Constructing the Speed-Time Graph for a Real-Life Scenario",
      "problem": "A particle accelerates from rest with a constant acceleration of $1\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}$ for the first $3$ hours of its motion. It then travels at a constant speed for the next $3$ hours before accelerating with a constant acceleration of $0.5\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}$ for other $3$ hours. Which graph represents the described motion?",
      "worked_solution": "We start the first line from the point $(0,0),$ since the particle starts from rest. Then, because the acceleration is $1\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2},$ we draw a line with slope $1.$ \n\nHowever, because the particle is only accelerating for the first $3$ hours, we stop the line at $t=3.$ At this time, the particle's speed has increased by $1\\cdot 3=3\\,\\text{km/h}.$ So, the first line segment goes from $(0,0)$ to $(3,3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/7a9fd32e5705860064e597fcf9308fee457063e060d3adede260f109ca488201.png)\n\nThe particle then travels at a constant speed for the next $3$ hours. So, the speed remains at $3\\,\\text{km/h}$ for the next $3$ hours, until $t=3+3=6.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/e4c7e47150bf1c205c243c6fa1d0f0e24b7a69f27a8461791d495eb7487cf4f3.png)\n\nAfter $t=3,$ the particle has an acceleration of $0.5\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2},$ so the slope of the third line segment is $0.5.$ The third line segment stops after $3$ hours. To figure out the velocity at this moment, we need to find the equation for the third line segment.\n\nSince the third line segment passes through the point $(6,3)$ and has a slope of $0.5,$ we can use point-slope form to write the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-{v}_{1} & =m(t-{t}_{1}) \\\\ v-3 & =0.5(t-6) \\\\ v-3 & =0.5t-3 \\\\ v & =0.5t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third line segment stops when $t=6+3=9$ hours, and the velocity at this moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =0.5t \\\\ v & =0.5(9) \\\\ v & =4.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third line segment stops at the point $(9,4.5).$ The final graph is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/a3fb0b9ebb6ef907a77c3d8ce139802dbd72fab04ea0bfc5db57e9f028c263b9.png)",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "image",
              "value": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/8dd2eefb2c2397f10d4c07853ef819b8682e448824bf6e26302d4a16bc02183c.png",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
