  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946119",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-28266",
      "knowledge_point_id": "51739685-2d53-5c9b-870b-b557ffdf2768",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Area of a Triangle by Solving for a Variable Given a Trigonometric Ratio of One Angle",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946119/assets/48cb6a248ab3ce706f26dc2e84e2feb337d509041c8b9756ea32fb3817aa097f.png)\n\nGiven that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A)=5,$ calculate the area (in square units) of $△ABC$ shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A)=5.$ Notice that relative to $∠A,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AC}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{BC}{AC} \\\\ 5 & =\\frac{2x+4}{x-1} \\\\ 5(x-1) & =2x+4 \\\\ 5x-5 & =2x+4 \\\\ 3x & =9 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =2x+4=2(3)+4=10, \\\\ AC & =x-1=3-1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
